#{[17592186531568 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531568 #edb/ref 1069 "q-119283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531568 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531568 #edb/ref 1071 "The growth rate of a watermelon production $P(t)$ in a certain land is jointly proportional to the watermelon production at time $t,$ measured in months, and the difference between the land's carrying capacity and the watermelon production. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2$ and the maximum possible watermelon production is $1\\,800,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531568 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2014fae0-a89c-5d1e-8735-16f0159dd06c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531568 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the watermelon production growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the watermelon production at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the watermelon production, the watermelon production follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1\\,800.$ Therefore, the watermelon production is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(1\\,800-P).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531587 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531587 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531587 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531587 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42788b85-30ff-5f36-a9fd-86e424b97617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531587 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot |3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531603 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186531508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531603 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531603 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531603 #edb/ref 1071 "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x+5$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531603 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a23334e-1f04-5511-928e-2caa0a2313aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531603 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x+5,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x-4.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and the endpoints $x=0,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints)\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & ={0}^{2}-4(0)+5=5 \\\\ f(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+5=1 \\\\ f(5) & ={5}^{2}-4(5)+5=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=1,$ so the global minimum is $1.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(5)=10,$ so the global maximum is $10.$\n\n![](images/9a/9a52ee757248f6b742af36af1e6af9e801cb8e5276b119e399b6fff36d75d0dd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531669 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531669 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1fbc467c-fa74-583d-ac77-d9d3baef5bc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531669 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531682 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531682 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34a6170e-0575-571a-ae56-b0c6b66f198b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531682 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531689 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531689 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57b8dfd6-9bac-59cf-ac44-c16486eda1c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531689 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531696 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531696 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8478a7a6-7989-5221-ae40-cc42a13728b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531696 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531702 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531702 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9cb89aa4-e9c5-5f00-b04f-4e9bc062f9b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531702 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186250282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531706 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad84a19f-114d-5fb3-b813-cbaf4567a4df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531706 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531709 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b55407ec-a2ee-5f9b-90f8-39bfad1179ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531709 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186354291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531712 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531712 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba33e601-868b-5386-9000-5cc1fbdaddc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531712 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d656c95b-f90d-539c-9dbd-3c2a66651730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dc9f379f-4b46-590e-aa26-910ad4ef4790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531723 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186531669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4403343-b5c6-5fde-abd9-266da3d46e06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186531626 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
